  "content": {
    "id": "p63-q5-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "5",
    "prompt": "Explain why $(x-2)(x^2-3x+3)=0$ has only one real solution.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$\\Delta=(-3)^2-4(1)(3)=-3<0$, so the quadratic has no real roots. The only real solution is $x=2$.",
      "worked": "For $x^2-3x+3$, $$\\begin{align*}\\Delta&=(-3)^2-4(1)(3)\\\\\\Delta&=9-12\\\\\\Delta&=-3<0\\end{align*}$$ Thus $x^2-3x+3=0$ has no real solutions. By the null factor law, the only real solution comes from $x-2=0$, giving $x=2$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 29
  },
